**Theorem 5.3 (Quantitative improvement and its distinct structural limit).**
For the unchanged original problem,

$$
\frac{129931}{250000}\le\varepsilon_1\le\lambda_{\rm mix},\qquad
\lambda_Q-\lambda_{\rm mix}>\frac7{12500000}.
\tag{5.9}
$$

The lower is the credited all-complex QJM bound. Neither the decoder
port nor the twenty-seven-flag port is a globally optimal port.
The necessary splitting-event relation (4.7)–(4.12) is independent
of the small endpoint improvement.

Proof. QJM Theorem 2.3 supplies the necessary lower for every
original complex competitor, while Theorem 5.2 supplies a legal
attaining sufficient channel. Subtracting the strict upper $u_m$
from the credited strict lower $\ell$ for $\lambda_Q$ gives
$\ell-u_m=7/12500000$, proving the improvement. Both constructed
channels are stationary and have only the keep event $N$ active.
Theorem 4.3 therefore excludes their global optimality. The mass
and active-event theorems use the comparison point and full TP,
not the mixing coefficient or the root isolation of either improved
port. They do not assert which splitting pair, signs, atom support
or globally positive certificate realizes the true optimum. $\square$

